---
id: org.dxos.plugin.computer
name: ComputerPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

Proof-of-concept, dev-only coding harness for Composer ("Coding (Dev)"): a bash tool and a
multi-string-replace edit tool, carried by a single vite server route (`vite dev` or `vite preview`)
that runs a shell script under a configured root. Nothing is mounted outside a vite server.

It also contributes the **Composer Plugin** project template: a project whose agent uses these tools to
build a TypeScript Composer plugin on this machine and offer it back to load (QA-2).

## Extensions

| Term | URI                        |
|------|----------------------------|
| `op` | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`      |
| `test` | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`  |

## Operations

```mdl
op bash
  key: org.dxos.operation.computer.runBash
  desc: Runs a shell script under the harness root and returns its output.
  input:
    script: string
    cwd?: string         # relative to the harness root
    timeout?: number     # ms, clamped by the host
  output:
    stdout: string
    stderr: string
    exitCode: number     # -1 when killed
    success: boolean
    signal?: string
    timedOut: boolean
    truncated: boolean
    cwd: string          # absolute directory the script ran in
    durationMs: number
  effects: [shell:exec]
```

```mdl
op edits
  key: org.dxos.operation.computer.applyEdits
  desc: Applies a batch of exact find/replace edits to files; all-or-nothing.
  input:
    edits: Array<{ path: string, oldString: string, newString: string, replaceAll?: boolean }>
    cwd?: string
  output:
    applied: boolean
    files: Array<{ path: string, replacements: number }>
    error?: string       # set iff applied is false
  effects: [shell:exec, fs:write]
```

## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an
invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by test and step number.

```mdl
test QA-1: Run a shell command and apply an edit
  desc: |
    The plugin has no ECHO types and no navtree/article surface — it is two raw tools
    (`bash`, `edits`) exposed to the assistant. There is no human gesture other than prompting
    the assistant, so `do` throughout is "ask the assistant to ...".
  actors: agent
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - plugin-computer is enabled — it is dev-only (gated on `isDev` for both availability and
      default) and never appears in a production build; enable it via the plugin registry in a
      dev environment, or confirm `isDev` is already on
    - the vite dev server's harness route is reachable (this plugin only functions there — "Nothing
      is mounted outside a vite dev server")
    - a scratch file the harness root can write to, not otherwise used by this space's QA fixtures
  steps:
    - name: Run a shell command
      do: Ask the assistant to run `echo qa-computer-check` via the Coding (Dev) tool.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.computer.runBash { script: "echo qa-computer-check" }
      capture: ran
      expect: the tool reports success with stdout containing "qa-computer-check" and exitCode 0
      assert: return $ran.success === true && $ran.stdout.includes('qa-computer-check');
    - name: Write a scratch file
      do: Ask the assistant to create a scratch file with known content, via the bash tool (the
        edits tool only replaces existing text, so seeding needs a shell write first).
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.computer.runBash { script: "echo 'before' > qa-computer-scratch.txt" }
      expect: the file now exists under the harness root containing "before"
    - name: Apply an edit
      do: Ask the assistant to replace "before" with "after" in that file.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.computer.applyEdits { edits: [{ path: "qa-computer-scratch.txt", oldString: "before", newString: "after" }] }
      capture: edited
      expect: the file's content is now "after"
      assert: return $edited.applied === true && $edited.files.some((f) => f.path === 'qa-computer-scratch.txt' && f.replacements > 0);
  after:
    - name: Remove the scratch file
      do: Ask the assistant to delete the scratch file.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.computer.runBash { script: "rm -f qa-computer-scratch.txt" }
      expect: the file no longer exists under the harness root
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Build a plugin with an agent from the Composer Plugin project template
  desc: |
    The demo script for the Composer Plugin project template (`src/templates/composer-plugin.ts`): an agent
    works one parent task and its four subtasks on this machine — read the guide, write a TypeScript plugin,
    build it, offer it — and the reader loads the result. The plugin adds a "World Clock" group to each
    space's navtree with a "Clocks" page under it: a world map from plugin-map above a row of live clocks,
    kept in a `Clock` ECHO object. Walked by the autocue flow
    `packages/plugins/plugin-computer/autocue/composer-plugin.mjs`.
  actors: both
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open (the default "My Space" is fine)
    - Composer is a bundled build served by `vite preview` on :4173 against EDGE preview — loading by URL
      needs the bundle's import map, and EDGE production rejects the AI requests
    - plugin-projects and Coding (Dev) are enabled; the template is contributed by Coding (Dev), so the
      create dialog offers it only when that plugin is on
    - the Assistant's default remote model is DeepSeek V4 Pro
    - no plugin named "World Clock" is loaded, no "Composer Plugin" project is left from an earlier run, and
      `temp/plugins/world-clock` and `out/composer/plugins/world-clock` do not exist, so the agent starts from
      an empty folder (the autocue flow's first setup step restores all of this)
  steps:
    - name: Create a project from the Composer Plugin template
      do: Click + on the space, choose Project, pick the "Composer Plugin" template from the list, title it "World Clock" and click Create, then open the project's folder in the navtree.
      capture: project
      expect: a "World Clock" project opens, and its navtree folder shows what it holds
    - name: Open the project's tasks
      do: Click the project's Tasks tab.
      expect: |
        one parent task, "Build the World Clock plugin", with four subtasks under it, all to do — read the
        guide, write the plugin, typecheck and build, offer it
    - name: Select the parent task and assign it to the agent
      do: Tick the parent task's checkbox, then click "Assign selected tasks to agent".
      expect: |
        the project's pipeline chart appears and a chat named "Build the World Clock plugin" holds the parent
        and its four subtasks
      note: Delegating a parent hands over its whole subtree (DelegateTaskToChat collects it), parent first.
    - name: Open the agent session
      do: Open the plank's companion and pick its Assistant tab.
      expect: the companion shows the delegated chat, already working from its opening turn
    - name: Show the chat's tasks, then hide them
      do: Click the prompt's tasks toggle, leave the checklist open for a few seconds, then close it.
      expect: the checklist lists the parent task and its four subtasks
      note: The checklist starts collapsed (it is the assistant's working state), so it is opened on purpose.
    - name: Open the Trace panel
      do: Click Trace in the right rail.
      expect: the Trace panel opens pinned to its latest entries, which follow the agent's work
    - name: Select each task in turn
      do: Click each of the first four task rows in the project, a second apart.
      expect: the companion follows the selection, showing each task's detail
    - name: Return to the chat
      do: Pick the companion's Assistant tab.
      expect: the delegated chat is back, still working
    - name: Wait for the agent to offer the plugin
      do: Watch the subtasks tick off.
      expect: all five tasks are done and the chat shows a "Load plugin" prompt naming World Clock
      note: |
        Takes up to twenty minutes. The model matters: with Composer's stock default (Claude Sonnet 5) the
        delegated chat stayed silent, which is why `given` sets DeepSeek V4 Pro.
    - name: Load the plugin
      do: Click Load on the prompt.
      expect: World Clock loads without an error toast, but stays off — the prompt only loads it
    - name: Enable the plugin in the registry
      do: Close the companion and the context sidebar, open Plugins, wait a beat, then open Labs and turn World Clock on.
      expect: |
        the World Clock card is listed under Labs (its `dx.config.ts` tags it `labs`) with its toggle off,
        and turning the toggle on activates it (and plugin-map with it, which it depends on)
      note: |
        Enabling is deliberately a second step: the reader sees what the plugin is before it runs, which is
        why the prompt's LoadPlugin call passes `enable: false`.
    - name: Open the Clocks page
      do: Return to the space and click Clocks under the WORLD CLOCK group.
      expect: |
        the navtree keeps its own groups (CONTENT, SYSTEM) and gains a WORLD CLOCK group holding Clocks; the
        page opens as an article with an equirectangular world map above one clock card for the local
        timezone (date, ticking time, timezone) and an empty card of the same size with a large plus button
      note: |
        An earlier version of the guide taught a rail workspace, and the built plugin replaced the whole
        navtree with its own; the guide now teaches a navtree group.
    - name: Add two timezones
      do: Click the plus, pick a timezone from the select and save; do it again with a second timezone.
      expect: the row shows three clock cards sorted west to east, each ticking in its own timezone, with a pin on the map, and the plus card after them
      note: The timezones live on the space's `Clock` object, so they survive a reload.
    - name: Select the clocks on the map, then on the globe
      do: Click each clock in turn, then switch the map to the globe and click each clock again.
      expect: |
        the selected clock's card is outlined and its pin is highlighted; on the globe the earth turns about
        its axis to the selected clock's meridian
  after:
    - name: Delete the project
      do: Right-click the "World Clock" project in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [$project.id], target: $given.space }
      expect: the project and its tasks leave the navtree
    - name: Uninstall World Clock
      do: Open Plugins, then Labs, open the World Clock card's detail page and click Uninstall.
      expect: World Clock leaves the registry and its WORLD CLOCK group leaves the navtree
    - name: Remove the build folders
      do: Delete `temp/plugins/world-clock` and `out/composer/plugins/world-clock` under the Composer app.
      expect: both folders are gone, restoring the `given` for the next run
      note: The autocue flow's first setup step does this and the uninstall before each take.
```

```mdl
test QA-3: Build a plugin with an agent in the desktop app
  desc: |
    QA-2 in the native desktop app. There is no vite server there, so no Computer shell: the template's
    desktop variant (`desktopVariant` in `src/templates/composer-plugin.ts`) has the agent create a local
    sandbox with plugin-sandbox's Sandbox skill, link the toolchain of the source tree the app was built from,
    build World Clock in the sandbox's workspace, and offer the URL Publish Files serves the build at. Walked
    by the autocue flow `packages/plugins/plugin-computer/autocue/composer-plugin-desktop.mjs` through
    `driver.mjs --target tauri`.
  actors: both
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open (the default "My Space" is fine)
    - Composer is the desktop app, a release build whose frontend was bundled with `DX_ENVIRONMENT=dev` against
      EDGE preview — a dev bundle carries Coding (Dev) and the source root local sandboxes may read
    - plugin-sandbox's Backend setting is Local and its `dx-sandbox` helper is staged beside the app binary
    - plugin-projects and Coding (Dev) are enabled, and the Assistant's default remote model is DeepSeek V4 Pro
    - no plugin named "World Clock" is loaded, and no "Composer Plugin" project or "World Clock" sandbox is left
      from an earlier run (the flow's first setup step restores this)
  steps:
    - name: Create a project from the Composer Plugin template
      do: As QA-2 step 1.
      capture: project
      expect: a "World Clock" project opens, and its navtree folder shows what it holds
    - name: Assign the parent task to the agent
      do: As QA-2 steps 2 to 4 — open Tasks, tick the parent task, assign it, open the companion's Assistant tab.
      expect: the delegated chat holds the parent and its four subtasks and is working
      note: The project's skill is Sandbox, not Coding (Dev); its instructions name the source root it links.
    - name: Wait for the agent to offer the plugin
      do: Watch the subtasks tick off.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.queryObjects { typename: "org.dxos.type.sandbox", text: "World Clock" }
      capture: sandboxes
      expect: |
        a "World Clock" sandbox exists, all five tasks are done, and the chat shows a "Load plugin" prompt whose
        URL is `http://localhost:<port>/files/<key>/manifest.json`
      assert: return $result.results.length === 1
    - name: Load and enable the plugin
      do: As QA-2 steps 10 and 11.
      expect: World Clock loads from the sandbox's published directory and activates from Labs
    - name: Open the Clocks page and add two timezones
      do: As QA-2 steps 12 and 13.
      expect: the page shows the map above three ticking clock cards and the plus card
  after:
    - name: Delete the project
      do: Right-click the "World Clock" project in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [$project.id], target: $given.space }
      expect: the project leaves the navtree
    - name: Delete the sandbox
      do: Right-click the "World Clock" sandbox in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { refs: [{ "/": $sandboxes.results[0].dxn }], target: $given.space }
      expect: the sandbox leaves the navtree and its shell environment is torn down
    - name: Uninstall World Clock
      do: As QA-2's after step 2.
      expect: World Clock leaves the registry and its WORLD CLOCK group leaves the navtree
```
